---

## Context

provisionBrain plans the brain-layer scaffolding the AIDE pipeline
needs on first install. The framework's brain contract is exactly two
required hubs — a coding-playbook hub and a research hub — plus the MCP
wiring that connects the host's agents to whatever knowledge store
backs them. provisionBrain's job is to plan five InitStep items that
together stand up that contract: the user-editable `brain.aide` config
file, the brain root directory tree, the playbook-hub artifact, the
research-hub artifact, and the brain MCP server entry. It writes
nothing to disk — every step is a planning artifact the install
service's apply layer turns into bytes.

The load-bearing decision is where the byte-level content for those
artifacts comes from. The legacy module owned the launcher shape, the
playbook hub body, and the navigation hub body as inline TypeScript
constants in `index.ts`. The new shape moves all of that content into
the user's own `brain.aide` body and reads it back through
`parseBrainAide`. The default scaffolded `brain.aide` ships three
named body sections — `## Prose` (agent-facing usage instructions),
`## Playbook hub` (the bytes scaffolded as the playbook hub artifact),
and `## Research hub` (the bytes scaffolded as the research hub
artifact) — and the parser exposes each section as its own typed field.
provisionBrain reads `playbookHub` and `researchHub` to populate the
hub-artifact steps; the package no longer owns the bytes those steps
deliver.

The brain.aide path lives under `.aide/config/`, the host's user-owned
configuration directory established by the root spec. The ownership
rule is directory-wide: any file scaffolded into `.aide/config/` is
owned by the user the moment it lands, and neither aide_init nor
aide_upgrade overwrites, patches, or migrates anything inside that
directory on subsequent runs. brain.aide is the current inhabitant; the
convention is forward-compatible with future user-owned config files,
and provisionBrain inherits the structural rule rather than restating
a per-file invariant.

provisionBrain is also the one-place migration surface for the fixed
`brain` MCP key. Legacy installs that wrote the entry under the old
`obsidian` key are migrated here and nowhere else: when a legacy
`obsidian`-only entry is present, the MCP step reports
`would-overwrite` with key `brain`, so the next `/aide:upgrade` or
`/aide:init` moves the entry. The both-keys-present transitional state
is handled the same way. There is no runtime fallback to the legacy
key elsewhere in the codebase.

The framework's brain contract does NOT include a vault-root CLAUDE.md.
A previous shape returned a CLAUDE.md scaffolding step under the
heading "vault navigation"; that step has been retired. Users who want
a CLAUDE.md inside their brain are free to add one — it is their
brain — but the framework does not require it, and provisionBrain
emits no InitStep for it. The methodology guidance that used to live
in that file (wikilink crawling protocols, decision protocols,
where-to-find-things tables) lives in pipeline prompts, where the
strategist owns the prose, and is delivered to agents through the
methodology doc hub rather than baked into install code.

## Strategy

**Five planning steps in fixed order: brain config → brain root
directories → playbook hub → research hub → MCP entry.** Each is its
own InitStep with its own idempotency check, and the order is
load-bearing: brain config is planned first because every step
downstream of it derives content from the bytes it scaffolds. The hub
steps come before the MCP step because the parser pass that powers
both reads the same brain.aide; running it once after the brain config
step is resolved keeps the orchestrator's flow linear.

**brain.aide is the single source of truth for both launcher bytes and
hub-artifact bytes.** The load-bearing structural change is that the
default scaffolded brain.aide ships three named body sections — agent
prose, playbook-hub content, research-hub content — and every artifact
step downstream sources its `content` from the corresponding section
via parseBrainAide. The MCP step calls parseBrainAide (or its
in-memory string sibling for the cold-install path where brain.aide
has not yet hit disk) and then `interpolateArgs` to compute the entry's
command and args. The hub-artifact steps call the same parser and read
`playbookHub` / `researchHub` for their bytes. No artifact step
hardcodes a launcher shape, a playbook structure, or a research
structure as an inline TypeScript constant — every byte the apply
layer eventually writes for these artifacts traces back to a body
section the user owns and can edit. A user edit to any section flows
into the next install or sync automatically; the package no longer
competes with the user for ownership of any of these bytes.

**Body section names are `## Prose`, `## Playbook hub`, and
`## Research hub`; parsed fields are `prose`, `playbookHub`, and
`researchHub`.** This is the working convention for the section
vocabulary the parser enforces. The headings are user-readable and the
parsed field names are camelCased mirrors. The architect/implementor
finalizes the exact strings; if the architect prefers a different
casing or punctuation, the convention should still be a closed
enumerated set so the parser can return `malformed-body` on any
unknown heading rather than silently dropping it.

**The bundled-default-brain.aide template helper keeps its current
name and folder, but its responsibilities expand.** The helper at
`obsidianBrainAideTemplate/` now owns three things: the collapsed
two-field frontmatter, the platform-branched launcher invocation
(POSIX `npx ...` vs Windows `cmd /c npx ...`), AND the full byte
content of all three body sections including the playbook-hub body
and research-hub body. The name remains intentional because the
scaffolded template content is Obsidian-flavored — the prose names
the `mcp__brain__read_note` and `mcp__brain__search_notes` tools, the
hub bodies are written as Obsidian Markdown notes — and the helper
describes its content, not a code-level special case. Reasonable
alternates are `defaultBrainAideTemplate` or `bundledBrainAide`;
proposing them here so the architect can finalize. What is NOT
acceptable is a name that suggests Obsidian is a code-level branch
target (e.g., `connectors/obsidian/`); Obsidian is a default template,
never a dispatch key.

**Code identifiers and inline prose stay storage-agnostic.** No
identifier in `index.ts` — function name, variable name, parameter
name, file path constant, JSDoc word, log string — uses "obsidian",
"vault", "hub note", "wikilink", or "note". The two structural
concepts the module handles are "playbook hub" and "research hub".
How those hubs manifest in the user's chosen storage is brain.aide's
concern, not this module's. The bundled default brain.aide stays
Obsidian-flavored because Obsidian is the most common brain backend,
but the mental model the code expresses is the framework's storage-
agnostic structural shape, not Obsidian's specific shape.

**The legacy `obsidianMcpEntry()` export is removed.** That function
returned a hardcoded launcher object directly. Callers that needed it
now go through the template + `parseBrainAide` + `interpolateArgs`
pipeline. Consumers that previously imported `obsidianMcpEntry`
migrate to derive the entry from a scaffolded or existing brain.aide.

**brain.aide scaffolding is seed-semantic, and the rule is
directory-wide.** The brain.aide file is user-owned the moment it
lands on disk — the user may swap the launcher, retarget the inline
path, rewrite the prose, edit the hub bodies, or change the label. A
presence check at the expected path is the correct idempotency
signal. The module never returns `would-overwrite` for brain.aide;
doing so would replace a user's customized config with the package's
default on every subsequent init. The same rule applies to every
other file under `.aide/config/` should one ever be added: the
directory itself is the ownership boundary, inherited from the root
spec, with no per-file allowlist.

**Two different idempotency semantics coexist in this module.** The
brain config file, the playbook-hub artifact, and the research-hub
artifact use **seed-semantic idempotency**: a simple presence check.
If the file exists at its expected path, the step is `exists` — no
byte comparison, no drift detection against the brain.aide section
that originated it. These files are seeds the user evolves the moment
the brain comes alive; once they own the file, the bytes belong to
them, and the package never proposes replacing them. The brain MCP
prescription uses **canonical-derived idempotency**: the expected
entry is computed from the current brain.aide and compared against
what is in `.mcp.json`. Divergence means `would-overwrite` (the user
may have re-run init before syncing, or the brain.aide was edited),
and the migration branches for legacy `obsidian`-only and both-keys
states fire on the same path. The distinguishing question is who owns
the bytes after first write: for user-curated content, presence is
enough; for the derived MCP entry under `.mcp.json["brain"]`, the
current brain.aide is the source of truth.

**Migration semantics for existing installs.** Cold installs land in
the new shape automatically — the bundled template carries all three
body sections from day one. Existing installs whose brain.aide has
only `## Prose` (the pre-refactor body) will surface `malformed-body`
from parseBrainAide on the first run that requires the new sections,
because the parser's body vocabulary is closed and missing required
sections are a structured failure. The remediation is for the user to
add the two missing sections to their brain.aide; provisionBrain does
not silently patch the file under `.aide/config/` to add them, because
that would violate the directory-wide ownership rule. There is no
aide_upgrade carve-out for this transition; the strict-failure path
is intentional.

**Signature takes `projectRoot`, not just `brainPath` and
`mcpConfigPath`.** The pipeline needs the host project root to derive
the brain.aide path at `.aide/config/brain.aide`. Separating
`brainPath` (the brain root) from the project root (where brain.aide
lives) makes the module's contract explicit. The install orchestrator
passes all three values and the single caller update is mechanical.

## Good examples

A cold install on a fresh project. provisionBrain returns five
InitStep items in order:

**Step 1 — Brain config.** Status: would-create. Category: brain.
Name: "Brain config (brain.aide)". FilePath:
`.aide/config/brain.aide` inside the host project root. Content: the
full canonical brain.aide bytes from the bundled template helper,
which on POSIX looks like:

```
---
name: obsidian
mcpServerConfig:
  command: npx
  args:
    - "@bitbonsai/mcpvault"
    - "/home/user/brain"
---

## Prose

Your brain is an Obsidian vault. Use `mcp__brain__read_note` to open
files by their vault-relative path. Use `mcp__brain__search_notes`
for keyword queries across every note in the vault. ...

## Playbook hub

# Coding Playbook

## Task Routing

| Task domain | Section |
|-------------|---------|

## How to Use This Index

Read this note first. Each section links to a section hub that lists
its notes with keywords. ...

## Research hub

# Research

## Domains

| Domain | Notes |
|--------|-------|

## How to Use This Index

Research notes are filed by domain. Drill into the domain that matches
your task; do not flat-search across every domain. ...
```

(On Windows the `mcpServerConfig` block becomes `command: cmd` with
`args: ["/c", "npx", "@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "D:/notes/my-brain"]`.)
The frontmatter has exactly two top-level fields: `name` (a label
only; the package never branches on it) and `mcpServerConfig`. The
brain root path is inlined byte-for-byte in `args` — no `${rootPath}`
placeholder, no separate `rootPath` field, no `entryFile`, no `tools`
block. The body has exactly three sections — `## Prose`,
`## Playbook hub`, `## Research hub` — and any unknown top-level
heading would surface as `malformed-body` from the parser.

**Step 2 — Brain root directories.** Status: would-create. Category:
brain. FilePath: the brain root. Content: a JSON array of the
directory paths to create.

**Step 3 — Playbook hub artifact.** Status: would-create. Category:
brain. FilePath: the playbook-hub artifact's path inside the brain
root (e.g., `coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md` for an Obsidian
brain — the storage shape is dictated by the user's chosen backend,
not by this module). Content: sourced from
`parseBrainAideFromString(brainAideStep.content).playbookHub` — the
verbatim bytes of the `## Playbook hub` body section in the
brain.aide that Step 1 just planned. The bytes are NOT a copy of an
inline TypeScript constant in this module; they came from the
brain.aide section, full stop.

**Step 4 — Research hub artifact.** Status: would-create. Category:
brain. FilePath: the research-hub artifact's path inside the brain
root (e.g., `research/research.md` for an Obsidian brain). Content:
sourced from `parseBrainAideFromString(brainAideStep.content).
researchHub` — the verbatim bytes of the `## Research hub` body
section. Same parser pass as Step 3, different field, different
artifact path.

**Step 5 — MCP config (brain).** Status: would-create. Category:
mcp. Prescription derived from the same Step 1 content bytes:
parseBrainAide parses the template string, interpolateArgs walks
`mcpServerConfig.args` (which contains no `${...}` placeholders on
the default scaffold, so the args pass through unchanged), and the
resulting entry on POSIX is:

```
{
  command: "npx",
  args: ["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "/home/user/brain"]
}
```

(On Windows: `{ command: "cmd", args: ["/c", "npx",
"@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "D:/notes/my-brain"] }`.) The args list is
byte-identical to what the template wrote to disk — interpolateArgs
runs but has nothing to substitute on the default scaffold.

Re-running on the same project after the first install: all five
steps return `exists`. The brain.aide presence check fires (the file
is on disk under `.aide/config/`); the brain root directories are
present; the playbook hub and research hub artifacts are present
(presence-only check, no byte comparison against the current
brain.aide section); the MCP config `brain` key matches the derived
entry.

Running on a brain where the user has hand-edited their brain.aide
to retarget the brain root by changing the inline path string in
`mcpServerConfig.args`: the brain config step is `exists` (the file
is present under `.aide/config/`); the brain root directory step
reports based on the new path; the MCP step derives its expected
entry from the user's edited brain.aide, NOT from the bundled
template default — the user's customization flows through
automatically. The hub artifacts at the new brain root either exist
(if the user already moved them) and report `exists`, or are absent
and report `would-create` with content derived from the user's
current `## Playbook hub` and `## Research hub` body sections. An
advanced user who instead pulls the path into a (now custom)
top-level frontmatter field and references it in `args` via
`${myPath}` exercises the `${...}` interpolation surface;
`interpolateArgs` substitutes the value and the derived entry's args
carry the resolved string.

A migration run on a legacy install (entry previously written under
`obsidian` key, no `brain` key): the MCP step reports
`would-overwrite` with `prescription: { key: "brain", entry: <derived
from brain.aide> }`. The other four steps run their normal
idempotency checks. After apply, the legacy `obsidian` key is
replaced by the canonical `brain` key. The both-keys-present
transitional state takes the same `would-overwrite` branch with the
same prescription.

## Bad examples

A provisionBrain that hardcodes hub bodies as inline TypeScript
constants:

```
// Wrong — hub bytes outside the brain.aide body
const PLAYBOOK_HUB_TEMPLATE = `# Coding Playbook\n\n## Task Routing...`;
const RESEARCH_HUB_TEMPLATE = `# Research\n\n## Domains...`;

steps.push({ name: "Playbook hub", content: PLAYBOOK_HUB_TEMPLATE });
steps.push({ name: "Research hub", content: RESEARCH_HUB_TEMPLATE });
```

The package now owns two top-level `*_TEMPLATE` consts in `index.ts`
(one per hub). A user who edits their brain.aide's `## Playbook hub`
or `## Research hub` body to add their own routing rows or domain
entries finds the next install ignores their edit and lands the
package default again. This is the same regression as the legacy
launcher-bytes duplication the schema collapse already retired,
applied to a different kind of byte. Hub bodies belong in named
brain.aide body sections and nowhere else in this module.

A provisionBrain that scaffolds a vault-root CLAUDE.md as part of the
brain contract:

```
// Wrong — CLAUDE.md is no longer a framework requirement
steps.push({
  name: "Vault CLAUDE.md",
  filePath: join(brainPath, "CLAUDE.md"),
  content: VAULT_CLAUDE_MD_TEMPLATE, // navigation hub bytes
});
```

The framework's brain contract is exactly two required hubs (playbook
+ research). A vault-root CLAUDE.md is a user choice, not a framework
requirement, and the navigation methodology that used to live in that
file (wikilink protocols, decision protocols, where-to-find-things
tables) is now delivered through pipeline prompts. Reintroducing a
CLAUDE.md scaffolding step under any name — "vault CLAUDE.md", "brain
CLAUDE.md", "navigation hub" — restores the retired contract. Users
who want a CLAUDE.md in their brain add one themselves; the package
does not plan one.

A provisionBrain that uses storage-specific identifiers in code:

```
// Wrong — "vault" and "obsidian" in code identifiers
async function vaultExists(brainPath: string): Promise<boolean> { ... }
const VAULT_DIRS = ["research", "process/retro", "coding-playbook"];
const obsidianHubPath = join(brainPath, "coding-playbook", "coding-playbook.md");
```

The mental model these names express is Obsidian's, not the
framework's. A user whose brain is a Notion workspace or a plain
folder still needs the playbook hub and research hub to land
somewhere; the code that plans those landings should not assume the
landing is a `.md` file inside an Obsidian vault. The structural
concepts the module handles are "brain root" and "hub artifact" —
how those manifest in the user's chosen storage is brain.aide's
concern, expressed in the body sections the user owns. The bundled
default template can be Obsidian-flavored because Obsidian is a
common backend, but no identifier or string constant in `index.ts`
may name it.

A provisionBrain that embeds wikilink-navigation prose as an inline
constant:

```
// Wrong — methodology guidance baked into install code
const HUB_NAVIGATION_PROSE = `When reading any hub note, follow
[[wikilinks]] that are relevant to your task. Hub notes are
navigation, not content. Content notes are where depth starts...`;
```

Wikilink crawling protocols, hub-vs-content depth rules, decision
protocols ("use the study-playbook skill for X, search_notes for Y"),
and where-to-find-things tables are methodology guidance that lives
in pipeline prompts, not in install code. Baking it into a constant
in `index.ts` couples the install pipeline to a specific brain
navigation paradigm and forces every methodology revision to ship as
a code release. The strategist owns this prose; the install module
is allowed to plan that hub artifacts land on disk and to source
their bytes from brain.aide, but it does not own the navigation
methodology itself.

A provisionBrain that skips the brain.aide step and constructs the
MCP entry inline:

```
// Wrong — reconstructs launcher directly
const entry = obsidianMcpEntry(brainPath);
steps.push({ name: "MCP config (brain)", prescription: { key: "brain", entry } });
```

The user's brain.aide (which may have a custom launcher, a different
label, or a different inline path) is ignored. The package's compiled
constant wins every time, defeating the user-ownership model the
refactor was built around.

A provisionBrain that hardcodes the Windows launcher shape in
index.ts:

```
// Wrong — launcher bytes outside the template helper
const command = process.platform === "win32" ? "cmd" : "npx";
const args = process.platform === "win32"
  ? ["/c", "npx", "@bitbonsai/mcpvault", brainPath]
  : ["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", brainPath];
```

The canonical launcher bytes now live in two places. When the
template helper changes (say, a new flag is added to the args list,
or the default launcher is swapped out for something else), this
inline copy silently diverges and the scaffolded brain.aide no
longer matches what the MCP step would compute for a cold install.
The same rejection applies to any inline copy of the legacy
`obsidian-mcp` launcher — duplicating launcher bytes outside the
template is the bug, not the specific launcher being duplicated.

A provisionBrain that returns `would-overwrite` for the seed
brain.aide:

```
// Wrong — byte-compares a user-owned config file
const disk = await readFile(brainAidePath, "utf8");
const template = bundledBrainAideTemplate(brainPath);
if (disk !== template) {
  return { name: "Brain config (brain.aide)", status: "would-overwrite", content: template };
}
```

The user edited their brain.aide to point at a new brain root. On
the next init, they are asked "overwrite your brain config?" A
reflexive Enter replaces their custom inline path with the stale
default from the template. This is data loss from a pattern that
only looks safe — and the same failure mode applies to any future
file scaffolded under `.aide/config/`: the directory boundary is the
ownership signal, and no code path in this module is allowed to
return `would-overwrite` for any path inside it.

A provisionBrain that scaffolds a default brain.aide using
`${rootPath}` substitution:

```
// Wrong — default scaffold exercises the advanced-user interpolation surface
return bundledBrainAideTemplate(brainPath); // emits args: ["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "${rootPath}"]
```

The default scaffold's job is to produce a working brain config that
the user can read top-to-bottom without learning a templating DSL.
Substitution is the opt-in path for advanced users who want to DRY a
value across positions; baking it into the default forces every user
into the advanced workflow before they have ever touched their
config.

A brain.aide scaffolded with intent-spec frontmatter fields:

```
---
scope: .aide/config/brain.aide # Wrong — no scope in brain.aide
intent: configures the brain   # Wrong — no intent paragraph
outcomes:                      # Wrong — no outcomes block
  desired:
    - brain is wired
name: obsidian
...
```

Every downstream agent that reads this file now has to decide which
rules apply — the brain.aide config rules or the intent-spec rules.
The distinction between config files and intent specs is load-bearing
and must be enforced at the scaffolding layer.

A brain.aide scaffolded with extra frontmatter fields beyond the
collapsed schema:

```
---
name: obsidian
connector: obsidian            # Wrong — field renamed away; `name` is the only label
rootPath: D:/notes/my-brain    # Wrong — path lives inline in args, not as its own field
entryFile: CLAUDE.md           # Wrong — no code consumes this
mcpServerConfig:
  command: npx
  args: ["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "${rootPath}"]
tools:                         # Wrong — interface contract lives in the prose body
  read: mcp__brain__read_note
  search: mcp__brain__search_notes
---
```

Each removed field is validation theater — a slot the user is nagged
to fill in with no code path that consumes the value once filled.
The schema collapse is load-bearing: every frontmatter field must
justify itself by naming a real runtime consumer (sync writes
`mcpServerConfig`; the user and agent read `name` as a label). The
agent-facing interface (which MCP tool names to call, where to start
reading, how to follow links) lives in the prose body where the
agent actually reads it.

## References

- `.aide/intent.aide` — Parent spec: brain.aide as the single source
  of truth for brain configuration, the collapsed two-field schema
  (`name` + `mcpServerConfig.{command, args}`), the
  `@bitbonsai/mcpvault` default launcher, the `.aide/config/`
  directory ownership boundary, the sync contract as the only
  mutation surface for `.mcp.json`, the no-substitution-in-the-
  default-scaffold rule, and the outcomes.undesired rule that
  brain.aide must not borrow intent-spec frontmatter conventions or
  add validation-theater fields. Informed the directory-wide
  ownership invariant, the schema-minimum-equals-maximum rule, and
  the no-connector-branching constraint that drives storage-agnostic
  identifier naming in this module.
- `src/service/parseBrainAide/.aide` — Sibling spec: the parser that
  reads brain.aide and returns typed frontmatter plus three named
  body fields (`prose`, `playbookHub`, `researchHub`), and the
  interpolateArgs helper that walks `mcpServerConfig.args`
  substituting `${...}` references against frontmatter values.
  provisionBrain consumes parseBrainAide for both the MCP step's
  prescription AND the byte content of the playbook-hub and
  research-hub artifact steps — every artifact's `content` field
  traces back through this parser to a brain.aide body section,
  never to an inline TypeScript constant. Informed the strict-
  failure migration semantics for existing installs whose
  brain.aide is missing the new sections (parseBrainAide returns
  `malformed-body`).
- `src/service/install/provisionBrain/obsidianBrainAideTemplate/` —
  Sibling helper: the bundled default brain.aide template. Now owns
  the collapsed two-field frontmatter, the platform-branched
  launcher invocation, AND the byte content of all three body
  sections (`## Prose`, `## Playbook hub`, `## Research hub`).
  Informed the strategy decision that this helper's responsibilities
  expand to include hub bodies, and that the helper name remains
  intentional because its content is Obsidian-flavored — not because
  the package has any code-level branch on Obsidian.
- `.aide/docs/brain-aide.md` — Methodology doc: brain.aide file
  format, frontmatter field semantics, the body section vocabulary
  (`## Prose`, `## Playbook hub`, `## Research hub`), the two
  substitution surfaces (args at install time, body sections
  verbatim at agent and install time), and the divergence from
  intent-spec conventions that makes scope/intent/outcomes fields
  explicitly absent.
